WS73 hardware-free USB regression
================================

Run from a clean committed kernel worktree on an x86-64 Linux host with
Python 3, GCC/glibc, Clang/LLD, make, QEMU and permission to use KVM.
Reserve four build jobs and two VM CPUs within the shared machine budget.
These scripts do not install a kernel or load a host module. Never execute
the compiled init fixture directly on the host or add physical USB passthrough.

  python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/build.py
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/run.py --speed full
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/run.py --speed high
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/run.py --phase boot --speed full
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/run.py --phase boot --speed high
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/run.py --phase download --speed full
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/run.py --phase download --speed high
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/check-supervisor.py <full-speed-record>

Do not use python -O: assertions form part of the verification and optimized
execution is explicitly rejected. The kernel is built from allnoconfig plus
usb.config; every requested configuration line must be satisfied. No earlier
test archive or private baseline configuration is needed. The build output is
fixed at ../.dev/ws73-upstream/build-virtual-usb relative to the source tree;
a shared build.lock excludes the other WS73 build/test scripts. Observation
hardware kernels in build-observe are not touched. The accepted build record
pointer and timestamped artifacts live under ../.dev/ws73-upstream.

The runner requires the build's clean source commit to match the current
worktree, including tools and documentation. Rebuild after committing changes.
It checks the exact required artifact set and hashes before starting QEMU,
including the BLE endpoint, runtime-link, shared USB IO and download-test objects. The fixture leaves
publication gated; having those objects does not mean a BLE child was exercised.
ACPI and PCI are required for this Q35 guest to power off: ACPI's default
PCI_Config region handler depends on PCI. Guest shutdown must terminate QEMU
successfully; a fixture PASS line followed by a process timeout is a failure.
This shutdown configuration is not WS73 device PM qualification.

The VM has no host USB, disk or network connection. It uses FunctionFS and
dummy_hcd to enumerate synthetic runtime devices, with separate kernel and
fixture serial logs. Each speed exercises 17 cases covering identity/version,
notifications, IRQ faults, injected disconnect and callback drain. A process
timeout kills only the VM started by the runner. Source, configuration,
tool, runtime library, kernel and fixture hashes accompany each attempt.
The negative checker corrupts a verified full-speed log in 32 ways and checks
five invalid build manifests (stale source, failed build, missing/extra artifact
and wrong hash). Every corruption must be rejected. It does not exercise a
device. Run it once for each phase's full-speed PASS record. Boot mode checks
22 log corruptions and the same five invalid build manifests. Download mode
checks 28 log corruptions and five invalid build manifests.

The boot phase enumerates two-endpoint synthetic devices in 14 cases. It checks
the exact fixed READM command, U/USB (including zero), E/USB, S/SDIO and E/UART
observations, replies of 0/1/3/5/512 bytes, a withheld reply timeout, removal
while awaiting a reply and fresh U/E instances after removal. Decoding S/SDIO
or E/UART does not enable those transports. Thirteen cases also check that no
extra OUT command arrives during a 100-ms window before removal. FunctionFS
synchronous reads are interrupted and drained by a periodic signal timer;
O_NONBLOCK alone does not bound queued IO. These are live USB transfers in
dummy_hcd, not proof that a real cold chip exposes the strap register.

The download phase uses the separate synthetic ID ffff:3734 and a test-only
USB driver that also requires the dummy_hcd controller. It calls the production
FILES/QUIT executor and shared URB/READY-wait helpers with explicit synthetic
views of 32769/32/32 bytes. The peer independently checks commands, all payload
bytes and QUIT. Seven cases per speed cover success, bad READY, bad FILES OK,
timeout, removal at READY/payload admission, then a fresh successful instance.
Five cases check no extra OUT for 100 ms; every instance must report callback
drain. Successful QUIT means WAIT_RUNTIME only. No runtime instance is started.

The fixture reads the actual endpoint descriptor and sizes its first payload
read to the negotiated 64/512-byte packet. A smaller high-speed read can
truncate the transfer and overflow FunctionFS. Wire timing includes scheduling;
the test driver separately measures the actual shared wait call and the
supervisor requires all ten waits to be at least 5000000 ns. The production
parent's boot transfer limit remains 32768 bytes, with no runtime ZLP flag.

This phase does not request original firmware, validate hashes, perform RAM/XO
preparation or prove device identity. Those contracts have separate tests;
this synthetic executor is not an alternative physical loading path. These
USB tests do not execute armed startup/customization, BLE class-control, HCI
or Wi-Fi, nor qualify physical U/E, RF, PM or peers. Missing resources, failed
configuration dependencies, malformed logs and unexpected driver records fail
the run rather than broadening the accepted result.
